bly each of these markers deserves  its own  symposium with multiple talks but i  won't go into that i'll just point out  that  we can mark for each of these markers  here's  a dual mark with t cells along with the  aux 40 marker  here's the lag three marker  here's jitter um again  mark's importance cell subsets here's  vista or v7h5 along with  cd3  uh here's uh vista by itself without the  cd3 cell so you can see that there's  actually a range of expression from low  expressors to high expressors of vista  here's pepin h4 much less common but  definitely  scattered cells which are b7 h4 positive  here  here's b7h3 expressed  we think more on antigen presenting and  dendritic cells  interestingly here's tim3 tim3 you kind  of have to dial up the signal to see the  brightest cells  and we think it's probably the brightest  cells that are the most important  so all of these immune synapse pathways  are  part of the future i think of  immunotherapy and biomarkers for  immunotherapy  all right so lastly i want to tackle  sort of this pre-cancer problem in  breast cancer um the pre-cancer  problem i broke down into four  categories  the over diagnosis pro  problem i showed you is largely due to  over diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in  situ  and so we really need to do a better job  of telling which duct carcinoma in situ  is and is not going to progress i also  think that  some of the highest risk invasive  cancers really don't even  have that detectable intraductal  component  dcis is a heterogeneous array of  diseases but they all have this  increased risk of invasive cancer and  my colleague laura esserman will tell  you that  the the evidence does not support our  current standard of  care our current standard of care is  surgical therapy often followed by  radiation therapy  to completely eradicate the part of the  ductal tree that harbors this  intraductal neoplasm  and i really like this picture this is a  picture of  dcis kind of at its worst it's in action  you can see that it's not a simple  question  to know how the micro invasion  here at the bottom left at sort of  between  seven and nine o'clock is occurring you  can see that there's  necrosis in the center of this dcis and  you can see inflammatory cells scattered  around the periphery what are those  inflammatory  cells doing are they now permitting or  are they  suppressing the invasion of dcif  and so more and more we're looking at  the immune system  as a way to predict risk and response  and if we  begin to look at the inflammatory  response around dcis  you can see that we definitively have  different patterns in different dcis  cases  so the cases are listed at the top  conventional markers like her2  positivity and er positivity  are given at the uh at the top bars  there with  her2 being that bright blue her2  negative being light blue you can see  her2 expression is very common  we're going to come back to that very  common in dcis much more common  in dcis than it is in invasive cancer er  positivity very common also in dcis but  if we look at these  now her2 positive over at the left edge  and we look at the inflammatory response  these have some of the highest levels  of cd8 infiltration  but even these are broken down into  subgroups  of three or four cases each which have  um stromal pdl1  cd868 cells that express pdl1 at the far  left  versus macrophages for cd68 that  don't express pdl1 in that box  um two steps to the right and then  in the in between them high cd8 cells  but very few macrophages  there are also this group at the far  right that have very little inflammation  almost no t cells and no macrophages  infiltrating in or around in the stroma  the dcis cases at the far far  right there in the middle here's  another group that's somewhat  interesting with very little immune  response  but the dcis cells themselves express  pdl1  and so um at the right  no inflammation but it's not we don't  think because of pdl1  but in the middle here's a