nvasive  cancer in this model system  some of these are metastatic and some  are not they segregate  and it gives you a unique opportunity to  study  those pre-cancerous lesions that are the  most important  versus those that are the most innocuous  in another model system  the stat one knockout mouse which was  made  as a way to reduce natural killer t  cell function there's a nice um  cell article from the shriver lab that  points out that  these stat one knockout mice are  susceptible to  obligate intracellular pathogens like  listeria challenge  but it turned out these mice also  develop  mammary tumors and they developed a  unique pattern of mammary tumor that  we think is very analogous to  age-related  er positive breast cancer which is  the most common type of breast cancer  and also  despite an overall good prognosis in the  category still  the number one cause of breast cancer  death and we asked whether  this was because of the natural killer  t-cell defect associated with a  difference in inflammation  whereby you might hypothesize that  women as they age have reduced  ability to be effective at  immunosurveillance or immunosuppression  of  these age-related er positive breast  cancers and i invite you to look at this  paper in detail  very interestingly it showed that the  tumor cells  express chemo attractants for  macrophages but  also express chemo  repellents for macrophages and this  resulted in  macrophages that come close to the  cancer but then  are not able to attack the cancer or  prevent its spread  because of the chemo repellent effects  and those segregate into extracellular  vesicle fractions  and soluble fractions which are these  dot plots  that are very small down at the bottom  in panels gh and i  but i invite you to look at the camp at  the at the paper for more details  [Music]  so a few clues about how  dcis might progressing progress come  from her too  i think her2 expression it turns out  in dcis is seen  in at least 35 percent in this case  compared to invasive carcinoma at only  12 percent  so that i think provides a clue that  her2 might be recognized by the immune  system and might be  one of the ways that  the immune system prevents dcis at least  hurt to expressing dcis from invading  and so we asked a simple question  about how  might a dcis evade the immune system and  we think the answer is  at least partially related to  mis-expression of  isoforms of her2 and there are different  isoforms this is sort of less  well-known but is known in the  literature  that this pattern up at the very top  where you show that there's only  detection by the green antibody the  intracellular domain of her2  but there's complete loss of the  extracellular domain of her2  and we hypothesized that loss of the  extracellular domain of her2  while it still gives you her2 signaling  and oncogenic potential  reduces the ability of the immune system  to recognize  that her2 and additional patterns we're  seeing you do see some full length  um apparent full length but you also see  partial losses that can be subclonal  and you can see gradients of of loss of  the extracellular domain of her2 in that  pattern 4  at the bottom and that we  hypothesize may be due to  post-translational  cleavage events in her2 that are very  common  so going back to the mouse my colleague  josh schneider at duke was able to model  this in what's called the cranbow mouse  and this is unpublished data but we hope  it'll be published soon and you'll be  able to  reference the full paper but we were  able to engineer this mouse to express  the different isoforms  in the same mouse and then ask about the  outcomes of those mice  and very interestingly the p95 loss of  extracellular domain  does show a very different kind of  immune pattern  immune reaction and shows much more  invasiveness  if you do single cell rna sequence  analysis of those epithelial cells  and fibroblasts you can see that there's  a much greater impact of the